title: Dependencies
section: Orchestration
desc: Make one scenario or group wait for another, share data between them, and see why a scenario was skipped.
lede: Use a dependency when one test really needs another to have finished first, for example a scenario that uses an account created by an earlier one.
---
<h2>Depend on a scenario</h2>
<p>Give the scenario you depend on an id with <code>@id:</code>, then point at it with <code>@dependsOn:</code>.</p>
<cb lang="gherkin" title="features/orders.feature">Feature: Orders
  @id:create-order
  Scenario: Create an order
    When I create an order for "apple"
    Then I get an order number

  @dependsOn:create-order
  Scenario: Cancel the order
    When I cancel the order I just created
    Then the order status is "cancelled"</cb>
<p>"Cancel the order" starts only after "Create an order" has passed. If it failed or was skipped, "Cancel the order" is skipped too, and so is anything that depends on it.</p>
<aside class="callout note"><p class="callout-title">Ids</p><p>Without <code>@id:</code> a scenario gets an id from its file and its name, such as <code>features/orders.feature::create-an-order</code>. Add <code>@id:</code> when other scenarios point at it, so renaming the scenario does not break them. For an outline row the id is <code>name[1]</code>, <code>name[2]</code> and so on. Pointing at <code>name</code> depends on every row.</p></aside>

<h2>Soft dependencies</h2>
<p>A soft dependency orders the scenarios but does not skip anything when the earlier one fails.</p>
<cb lang="gherkin">@dependsOn:create-order:soft
Scenario: Clean up test orders</cb>

<h2>Depend on a group</h2>
<cb lang="gherkin">@dependsOn:group:setup
Scenario: Browse the catalogue</cb>
<p>This scenario waits for every member of the <code>setup</code> group. You can also set <code>dependsOn</code> on the group in the config, which gives every member the same dependency. See <a href="groups.html">Groups</a>.</p>

<h2>Order whole features</h2>
<p>When an entire feature file needs another file to have run first, tag the feature with <code>@requires:</code> and the path of the file. Every scenario in the tagged feature then waits for every scenario in that file.</p>
<cb lang="gherkin" title="features/orders.feature">@requires:setup.feature
Feature: Orders
  Scenario: Place an order
    Given I am logged in</cb>
<p>The path is read relative to the tagged file's folder, then relative to the project root, then as the end of a path (<code>setup.feature</code> finds <code>features/shop/setup.feature</code> if only one file ends that way). If two files match, bddr stops and asks for more of the path. A file cannot require itself, and two files cannot require each other. Put <code>@requires</code> on a single scenario or rule to limit it to that part of the file, and add <code>:soft</code> to wait without being skipped when the required file fails. File names with spaces are not supported in a tag.</p>

<h2>What happens when a dependency fails</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Dependency kind</th><th>When the dependency does not pass</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>@dependsOn:id</code></td><td>The scenario is skipped. The skip cascades to anything that depends on it.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>@dependsOn:id:soft</code></td><td>The scenario still runs.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>@requires:file.feature</code></td><td>Each scenario in the tagged feature is skipped when any scenario in the required file does not pass. With <code>:soft</code> they still run.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Group <code>dependsOn</code></td><td>The default is <code>onDependencyFailure: 'skip'</code>. Set <code>'run'</code> to run anyway, as a reporting group would.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2>Which results skip a dependent</h2>
<p>A hard dependency must end as <code>passed</code>. Every other result skips the scenarios that depend on it: <code>failed</code>, <code>pending</code>, <code>undefined</code>, <code>ambiguous</code> and <code>skipped</code>. This holds even with <code>--no-strict</code>, which only changes the exit code. A failing <code>@quarantine</code> scenario also skips its dependents, even though the build does not fail for it.</p>
<p>The skip reason in the report and the console names the scenario and its result, for example <code>dependency failed: create-order (failed)</code>. When several dependencies did not pass, the reason names the first one in the order the tags were written. With <code>--fail-fast</code>, scenarios skipped because the run stopped show the fail-fast reason instead.</p>
<p>With <code>--dry-run</code> nothing executes, so every scenario is reported as skipped without a dependency reason.</p>

<h2>Rules and edge cases</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>What bddr does</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>The same target is named as hard and as soft</td><td>The hard dependency wins.</td></tr>
<tr><td>A scenario depends on itself, or the tag has no value (<code>@dependsOn:</code>)</td><td>Ignored, with no message.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Two scenarios in different files have the same <code>@id</code></td><td>Plan error, exit code 2, naming both file and line. <code>bddr validate</code> reports it once.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>@dependsOn:name</code> where <code>name</code> is both an id and an outline</td><td>The exact id wins over the outline's rows.</td></tr>
<tr><td>A soft dependency whose target is not in the run</td><td>Dropped. Soft dependencies never add scenarios to a selection.</td></tr>
<tr><td>A hard dependency that your <code>-t</code> or tag filter would exclude</td><td>Still added to the run, because the dependent cannot run without it.</td></tr>
<tr><td>A cycle made only of soft dependencies</td><td>Still a plan error. Soft dependencies order scenarios, so a loop cannot be ordered.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Any plan error (unknown id, duplicate id, cycle)</td><td>Nothing runs. bddr does not run part of a plan.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Config group <code>dependsOn</code> names a group that is only used in tags</td><td>Plan error <code>unknown group</code>. Declare every group you depend on under <code>groups</code> in the config, even with an empty <code>{}</code>.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2>Run one scenario and what it needs</h2>
<p>When you select a scenario with <code>-t</code>, a name, or <code>file:line</code>, bddr adds the scenarios it depends on, including the files it requires, even from other files. Add <code>--no-deps</code> to run only what you selected.</p>
<cb lang="bash">$ npx bddr run features/orders.feature:9
$ npx bddr run features/orders.feature:9 --no-deps</cb>

<h2>Pass data to a later scenario</h2>
<p>A scenario can export a value, and a scenario that depends on it can read it. The value is copied, and it works across worker processes.</p>
<cb title="steps/orders.steps.ts">import { When, Given } from '@bddr/runtime';

When('I create an order for {string}', async (ctx, product: string) => {
  const orderId = await createOrder(product);
  ctx.export('orderId', orderId);
});

Given('the order created earlier', (ctx) => {
  const orderId = ctx.from('create-order').get<string>('orderId');
  ctx.state.set('orderId', orderId);
});</cb>
<aside class="callout warning"><p class="callout-title">Add the dependency</p><p><code>ctx.from(id)</code> throws if that scenario has not finished. If you see that error, add <code>@dependsOn:</code> for it.</p></aside>

<h2>Check the plan</h2>
<p>bddr reports unknown ids, duplicate ids, unknown groups and cycles such as <code>a -&gt; c -&gt; b -&gt; a</code> before anything runs, and exits with code 2. <code>npx bddr plan --plan-format mermaid</code> prints the dependency graph as a Mermaid diagram you can paste into a wiki. The HTML report shows the same graph after a run.</p>
